# ForgeAI
> The agent that builds agents. One paragraph and a folder of documents in; a tested, grounded agent and an honest report out, on small local models.
author: ForgeAI
accent: clay
// Generated by scripts/make_brief.py. Every number on these slides is read from a file in this repository.
// Present in ForgeAI Studio (forge ui -> The brief -> Present): ← → step · P autoplay · N notes · Esc stop.

## The challenge
Build the machine that builds the chatbot: interview the requester, refuse what the documents cannot support, generate the agent, write its tests, judge it, repair it, and report honestly. It must work on a brief it has never seen, on a small imperfect model.
```visual
metrics: Pipeline stages = 9 | Extensions = 6 | Demo runs committed = 2 | Unseen briefs benchmarked = 5
```
note: The real test is a brief and folder the factory has never seen, run unattended. Everything here was built for that, not for the two demos.

## One rule underneath everything
Small models are wrong often. So no claim a model makes is believed until code has checked it.
```visual
flow: Model claim -> Check in code -> Keep / downgrade / drop -> Recorded in the run
active: Check in code
```
> Quotes must exist word for word. Numbers must match exactly. Labels are checked against the documents. The judge is tested on answers whose verdict is known. Patches must prove they helped.

## The factory
Nine stages, each writing its artifact as it finishes, so a run can be inspected, resumed and replayed.
```visual
flow: Brief + docs -> Interview -> Groundability guard -> Agent -> Tests -> Judge -> Optimizer -> Report
active: Groundability guard
```
note: 21 prompt files, all committed and hashed into each run's manifest. 6,190 lines of Python, 1,473 lines of hand-written Studio front end.

## It asks before it builds
At most five questions, only the ones that would change the agent, each with a stated default so an unattended run still proceeds. Every answer's source is recorded.
```visual
metrics: Questions asked (HR) = 5 | Answered from requester notes = 2 | Defaults recorded as assumptions = 3 | Must-answer topics = 6
```
note: Requester notes are only used when the answer quotes them verbatim, so the stand-in cannot invent preferences.

## It refuses to build on sand
The HR brief asked for **Payroll and income-tax deductions and Health insurance claims**. The documents say nothing about them. The guard flags this before anything is generated, and the agent is told to decline instead of inventing.
```visual
metrics: Coverage = 58% | Supported topics = 3 | Gaps flagged = 2 | Model verdicts overridden by code = 0
```
> A 'supported' verdict counts only if its evidence quote exists verbatim in the documents. Below 25% coverage the factory stops and builds nothing.

## Tools have to earn their place
HR agent: **no tools**. The model proposed 2 tool(s) and code vetoed them: calculator (vetoed: the topic it would serve ('Payroll and income-tax deductions')); current_date (vetoed: no supported requirement mentions a need this tool serves).
Procurement agent: **csv_lookup, calculator, weather**. Topics needing live data (Weather outlook) are tool-backed, not gaps; Shipment tracking is a gap.
```visual
flow: Model proposes -> Precondition holds? -> Need evidenced in a supported topic? -> Not serving a gap? -> Attached
active: Need evidenced in a supported topic?
```
note: After evaluation, any tool the agent never used is removed if removing it costs no dev case.

## It writes its own exam, and distrusts it
Answerable, cross-document, rule-application, re-phrased, tool, out-of-scope and gap questions, generated from the documents. Code then drops the weak ones before anything is scored.
```visual
metrics: Cases kept (HR) = 29 | Dropped by self-checks = 16 | Answerable without the documents = 5 | Holdout never seen by the optimizer = 13
```
- Facts must be verbatim spans of the source; paraphrases are repaired to the exact span or dropped
- Rule-application tests record the tempting wrong value as a trap (the hotel in Pune, which is not a metro city)
- 'Must refuse' labels are checked against the corpus; edge cases never cross the dev/holdout split

## A judge that is judged
Code judges first: facts, numbers, refusals, citations. The model judge sees one fact at a time, and its quote must exist in the answer. Then the judge is audited on canaries whose verdict is known.
```visual
metrics: Canary accuracy = 100% | Fact verdicts decided by code = 57% | Verbosity effect = +0 pts | Position bias = none by design
```
note: The judge (gemma3) is a different family from the agent (qwen3). Swapping judge families changed no score: docs/MODEL_SELECTION.md.

## Diagnose. Patch one thing. Prove it.
Failures are classified by measurement: retrieval miss, hallucination, wrong refusal, prompt defect. Each targeted patch is gated on its cases plus a regression sample; an iteration survives only if dev strictly improves.
```visual
flow: Measure the failure -> One targeted patch -> Gate: fixes targets, breaks nothing -> Keep or revert
active: Gate: fixes targets, breaks nothing
```
- Kept: P1.1 `retrieval`, fixed 1, broke 0
- Rejected: 2 patch(es) that did not fix their targets, e.g. "Added instruction to ensure all relevant details, including full scope of coverage, are included."

## Results
Holdout first, with its 95% interval. HR 67-99%; procurement 58-96%. The report then states exactly what still fails and why.
```visual
metrics: HR holdout = 12/13 | HR dev = 16/16 | Procurement holdout = 11/13 | Procurement dev = 15/17
```
note: HR run 0.0 min, procurement run 0.0 min, on one laptop with local models.

## Factory vs a careful human
Same brief, documents, model, runtime and judge. Only the author of the agent definition differs.
```visual
metrics: Factory (one paragraph) = 28/29 | Hand-written by the developer = 26/29 | Paired test p-value = 0.62
```
> From one paragraph and zero human effort, the factory's agent matched or beat a hand-tuned one on the same tests - and the factory also flagged the gaps, wrote and verified the tests, and audited its own judge. The paired difference is not statistically significant, and the report says so.

## Briefs it had never seen
Built for briefs it has never seen: different domains and formats, plus one adversarial brief whose documents are unrelated.
```visual
metrics: Benchmark briefs = 5 | Correct outcome (build or stop) = 5 of 5 | Mean holdout of built agents = 88% | Adversarial brief = stopped at the guard
```
- it-helpdesk: holdout 11/12; tools none; gaps 0
- mismatch-adversarial: stopped (not groundable)
- saas-sales: holdout 7/8; tools csv_lookup, calculator; gaps 1
- university-handbook: holdout 8/11; tools current_date; gaps 1
- vessel-sms: holdout 10/10; tools weather; gaps 0

## Models chosen by measurement
One frozen eval set, re-run with different agents and judges. The best agent became the default.
```visual
metrics: qwen3:8b judged by gemma3:4b = 28/32 | qwen3:4b judged by gemma3:4b = 27/32 | qwen3:4b judged by qwen3:8b = 27/32 | gemma3:4b judged by qwen3:8b = 26/32
```
note: Every profile keeps exactly three models resident, which fits Ollama's defaults on Windows with no configuration.

